{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והיו לבשר אחד", :translit "ve-hayu levasar echad", :gloss "and they become one flesh"} {:he "והיו שניהם ערומים", :translit "ve-hayu shneihem arumim", :gloss "and they were both naked"} {:he "בבגדו", :translit "be-vigdo", :gloss "in his garment / in his betrayal"} {:he "בבגדה", :translit "be-vigdah", :gloss "in her garment / in her betrayal"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semuchin: textual adjacency of 'one flesh' (Gen 2:24) and 'both naked' (Gen 2:25) read as thematic link"} {:kind :other, :note "pun: בגד (garment) read also as בגד (betrayal), so 'clothed in his garment' = 'clothed in his betrayal'"} {:kind :other, :note "summary of Talmudic divorce law embedded as the allusion's referent"}], :frame {:text "The double meaning of the root בגד as both 'garment' and 'betrayal' is exploited to read Genesis 2:25 — 'they were both naked (ערומים)' — as meaning each spouse stands exposed by the other's betrayal. The immediately preceding verse, 'they become one flesh' (Gen 2:24), is thus linked: the marital bond is shattered by infidelity, obligating divorce with full ketubah payment to the wife. The law that a wife's betrayal forfeits her ketubah while a husband's betrayal obligates him to pay it appears in Ketubot 72a-b.", :sources ["Genesis 2:24" "Genesis 2:25" "Ketubot 72a-b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semuchin: textual adjacency of 'one flesh' (Gen 2:24) and 'both naked' (Gen 2:25) read as thematic link", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "pun: בגד (garment) read also as בגד (betrayal), so 'clothed in his garment' = 'clothed in his betrayal'", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "summary of Talmudic divorce law embedded as the allusion's referent", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:04:07.344343749Z", :model "glm-5.2"}